-- w-free, memory given back. w-alloc's bytes stay valid for the process's life, so every buffer taken
-- for one read was held until the process ended: 64 KiB per read of a descriptor, per round of a socket
-- read, per request the server answered, per directory listed, per entry the guard stat-ed, and an argv
-- and envp with their strings per spawn. That, and not the allocator, is what made w-alloc's out of
-- memory reachable, and it is why a server answering requests for days died of memory. with-bytes n f
-- (lib/world.cub) takes n bytes, runs f, gives them back and answers f's answer, and h-free-ptrs gives
-- back an argv with the texts in it. Every buffer whose use ends inside its own definition now goes
-- through it (h-put, h-read-fd, h-recv-all, h-cwd, h-wait, h-file-time, h-mkdir, h-unlink, h-list,
-- h-typed-list, h-dir-time, h-http, h-capture-with, h-run, s-read, os-staged). What is left allocates
-- once for the life of a process (s-addr, s-listen) or hands its address to its caller by design
-- (h-cstr, h-ptrs, h-file), so no allocation grows with the work done any more.
-- src/drivers.c (2026-11-18, Avik: "I am sick of things being wrong and I won't accept unproven code
-- in this repository anymore"): w-snapshot, the n bytes at an address taken as a byte string at the
-- moment the command is performed. %region-at copies the same bytes but copies when the copy is
-- demanded, which can be after the memory has been given back; a command is ordered by the commands
-- around it. On this rests with-region (lib/host.cub), which takes a buffer, lets the host fill it,
-- copies it into a Region n, gives the bytes back and answers a program over that region. What the
-- types then carry, with no argument about the code: every access is through Ptr n, whose second
-- component is the proof that the cell fits in n bytes, so no read of a filled buffer is outside it
-- (h-stat-time, h-cell0, os-size-time, and h-file-time, h-wait, h-capture-with, os-staged over them);
-- the program over the region is a function of the region and the system call's answer, so it is not
-- given the address and cannot keep it; and the copy precedes the free by the order of the commands.
-- What they do not carry: the filling step is handed the address, as it has to be to name the buffer
-- in a system call, so a step answering the address rather than the call's result would hand it on.
-- Every one written here answers a system call's result.
