-- src/syntax.c, src/lang.c (2026-11-22, Avik: "Anything that is blocking sane max throughout work
-- cannot be delayed"; "These behavioral policies you propose are a failure of judgment"): writers
-- that do not wait for one another. The pool of entries already merges entries live together at a
-- path by definition; a write worked against it by recording a whole module before its check,
-- following whatever was live when it landed, so writers took turns under one lock. Three additions
-- and one repair. `--with PATH FILE` on a read: PATH's text is FILE's for that run, and the run
-- records nothing of that path (src_text, w_file), so a change is checked before anything is
-- recorded. `--record PATH FILE --follows IDS`: the entry names the entries the text was made from;
-- when those are not the ones live at the path now, it is written beside them and the index's stamp
-- dropped, so the next run computes the index from the pool and merges the two or names them as
-- alternatives (e_bind, rec_follows). `--head PATH`: the live entries and the module at a path, read
-- as one. The repair: the index, a cache computed from the pool, is changed under a lock of its own
-- held for the moment of the change (idx_lock: index_sync, a record, --head), and a record's stamp
-- is the index's own with its entry in it (e_stamp_with), not the pool's read again, which could
-- name an entry another machine wrote and the index does not hold. And `--base MODULE` with a record:
-- when the text was made from other entries than are live, a name it changed that the path's module
-- changed differently is a conflict: nothing is recorded and the run says `conflict NAME` and ends
-- with 3, so two writers of one machine never leave a path holding alternatives.
