-- machine's change to a module applied here as a construction; `--export HASH KIND FILE`;
-- `--drop-view`, which removes the removed layer's files from a pool. Kept from the earlier change
-- of this day: `--with PATH FILE` (a text read in place of the source's, nothing of it recorded),
-- `--head PATH`, and the lock on the labels held for the moment of a change.
-- src/store.c, src/syntax.c, src/lang.c (2026-11-22, Avik: "Do it all", of what was left for a
-- definition to be held by its content and found without a module): a definition taken from the
-- store by its key. load_key (src/syntax.c): the keys its stored forms mention first, then its
-- forms read back (decanon) and given to the checked form's handler, which finds the certificate.
-- A data type is read back from its declaration kept beside its key (ser_decl, store_decl,
-- load_decl; the constructors' and fields' names pointed at the key, conof/NAME). Beside a
-- definition's key: its implicit mask with the instance bits, and whether it computes (x_checked).
-- A name the text being checked mentions that the machine does not hold is looked up among the
-- machine's labels and loaded (lk_label, lk_scan), so a check loads what it mentions. Its forms as
-- elaborated are kept beside the key too (forms), which is what reads back. A machine process kept
-- warm from before a rebuild counts itself stale (session_key). What is kept beside a key is kept in
-- the pool, written once (kobj_read, kobj_write), so every machine has it; `--attach` attaches each
-- name's text to its key. A root names an object of the names (names_make, names_take): each name's key
-- and each constructor's type, so a machine that has checked nothing finds a definition by its name.
-- src/store.c, src/syntax.c, src/lang.c (2026-11-22, Avik: "Do it all", continued): one declaration
-- written on its own, in no module. `--read FILE --keep NAME TEXT` (src/lang.c): FILE is the
