-- HYPERACTIVE_PLACE set and no pool fetches the root's packs itself, each kept only when its hash is
-- its name. (2) The object of the names says, of each name held now, its key, its text and the module
-- that holds it (`b`, `t`, `m`), and root_take applies it after the modules, so a name declared in
-- two modules is pointed where the root says. (3) Reading a module moves no other module's label:
-- names_bind (src/store.c, bind_rel) binds nothing for a name another module holds, and w_file binds
-- a name when its module claims it. (4) canon (src/store.c): a code literal's reference to a
-- definition or a data type, a number in this machine's tables, is written in a key as that one's
-- key. Found by sending a root to a place and taking it in a checkout with no pool: the root said
-- after taking differed from the root sent, by names of lib/x86.cub whose term holds `code X`
-- (#2261 on one machine, #2412 on the other). After (1)-(4) the two roots are one hash. Still by
-- number, and so still a key that what was loaded before can change: a definition that observes
-- the number itself (step-d in lib/elf.cub takes a data type's number out of a code literal).
-- src/syntax.c, src/lang.c (2026-11-22, Avik: "get it all done"): a module of the work sees a name
-- no module holds. lk_label takes, in a module, only a definition written on its own or a
-- constructor or field of a type written on its own (free-con/NAME, written when the type is
-- labelled and said by a root, `f`), and remembers a name it did not find. A name that already
-- holds the very definition or type being declared (the same key, taken from the pool before the
